Equivalence Groups Found
└── Modbus TCP client for reading/writing holding registers to industrial field devices
    ├── Candidates : pymodbus, pyModbusTCP, modbus-tk
    ├── Used APIs  : ModbusTcpClient, client.connect, client.read_holding_registers, client.write_register, rr.isError, rr.registers
    ├── Pitfalls   : pymodbus returns response objects with .isError() and .registers; pyModbusTCP typically returns plain lists or None on error, requiring 
    │   rewritten error-handling logic; modbus-tk uses a different Master/Query API (execute() with function codes) rather than named methods like 
    │   read_holding_registers; unit/slave id parameter naming and position differs across libraries; connection lifecycle (connect/close) semantics differ, 
    │   e.g. auto-reconnect behavior
    ├── Constraints: requires_connector
    └── All three libraries implement Modbus TCP master/client functionality capable of reading and writing holding registers over TCP, which is the core 
        operation performed in this file (read_holding_registers, write_register). pyModbusTCP is TCP-focused like the current usage; modbus-tk also supports 
        TCP master mode. minimalmodbus was suggested but is primarily a serial (RTU) library with a fundamentally different transport model and lacks a native 
        TCP client interface matching this file's usage, so it is a weaker fit and requires more architectural change than the others.

